.TH RESMAN 8 "September 2026" "v1.31.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
resman \- Dynamic CPU/RAM/IO resource manager for Linux using cgroups v2
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B resman
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B resman
is a daemon that monitors system and per\-user CPU, RAM, and block I/O usage.
When configured resource thresholds are exceeded, it dynamically applies CPU
quota and weight, memory high and maximum limits, and I/O bandwidth and IOPS
limits to selected users. It uses the Linux cgroups v2 CPU, memory, and I/O
controllers to enforce those limits.
.PP
The daemon operates in control cycles configured via
.B POLLING_INTERVAL
and makes decisions based on configurable CPU, RAM, and I/O thresholds to
activate, maintain, or remove resource limits.
.SH FEATURES
.IP \(bu 2
Dynamic CPU limiting based on configurable thresholds
.IP \(bu
Dynamic RAM limiting with soft/hard limits (memory.high/memory.max)
.IP \(bu
IO bandwidth and IOPS limiting (io.max)
.IP \(bu
Automatic user detection and cgroup management
.IP \(bu
Blackout timeframes support (BLACKOUT)
.IP \(bu
User filtering with regex support (USER_INCLUDE_LIST, USER_EXCLUDE_LIST)
.IP \(bu
Process exclusion list (PROCESS_EXCLUDE_LIST). Excluded processes remain in
observed totals but do not contribute to CPU, RAM, or I/O decisions and are not
moved into limited cgroups. Matching uses only the /proc/PID/exe basename;
/proc/PID/comm is display-only because a process can rewrite it. If executable
identity is unavailable, the process remains enforceable and resman reports an
explicit error. Active limits reconcile membership every control
cycle. Newly excluded processes return only to a captured origin for the same
PID start time; without a safe origin they remain constrained and an error is
reported.
.IP (bu
Process ingress into a ResMan-owned cgroup is allowed only when the process PID
namespace equals the namespace cached from /proc/self/ns/pid at daemon startup.
The identity is verified again immediately before the cgroup.procs write. A nested
or unreadable PID namespace is skipped, logged once per user operation, and counted
without removing the process from observation or decision accounting. This guarantee
requires resman to run on the host, or with the documented host PID namespace. Restore
and recovery bypass the ingress guard because they remove an existing constraint.
.IP
Because decision accounting remains UID-wide, a refused nested workload can keep a
mixed UID above the release threshold. Host-namespace processes already controlled by
resman then remain constrained until total UID usage falls, even though their limit
cannot reduce the nested workload's consumption. The
.B pid_namespace_boundary
warning identifies the UID and bounded skip counts; correlate it with active-limit
state. The host-level
.B resman_cgroup_ingress_skipped_total
counter reports how often ingress was refused but deliberately has no UID label.
.IP \(bu
Threshold time window to prevent false activations (CPU_THRESHOLD_DURATION)
.IP \(bu
Username resolution cache for improved performance (USERNAME_CACHE_TTL)
.IP \(bu
SQLite metrics database for historical data (METRICS_DB_*)
.IP \(bu
MCP server with a mechanically verified tool inventory for AI assistant integration
.IP \(bu
Prometheus metrics export with hostname and server_role labels
.IP \(bu
LDAP/NIS username resolution support (requires CGO)
.IP \(bu
Workload pattern detection for adaptive limits (AUTODETECT_PATTERNS)
.IP \(bu
IO starvation auto\-remediation (IO_REMEDIATION_ENABLED)
.IP \(bu
Separate timeout configuration for cgroup operations
(CGROUP_OPERATION_TIMEOUT), the complete daemon shutdown sequence
(DAEMON_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT), and the MCP server shutdown step
(MCP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT)
.IP \(bu
PSI event-driven control cycles for reduced polling overhead (PSI_EVENT_DRIVEN)
.IP \(bu
Performance optimizations: gopsutil process scanning, batch PID moves, regex cache
.IP \(bu
Graceful shutdown with proper resource cleanup
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
Specifies the configuration file path. Default:
.I /etc/resman/resman.conf
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Displays program version and exits.
.SH PLATFORM REQUIREMENTS
.B resman
requires Linux kernel 4.18 or later with cgroups v2 enabled.
.PP
At startup, resman probes a real child cgroup for the interfaces required by the
configured features. CPU Points enforcement requires both
.B cpu.max
and
.BR cpu.weight .
When
.B RAM_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
is configured,
.B memory.max
is mandatory; when
.B IO_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
is configured,
.B io.max
is mandatory. A controller name in
.B cgroup.controllers
is not sufficient. A missing required interface causes startup to fail with the
feature, controller, and interface named. The
.B cpuset
controller is optional for CPU Points quota and proportional-weight enforcement.
.PP
Capability discovery is a startup snapshot. If
.B memory.max
or
.B io.max
was unusable during that probe, a later reload that requests the corresponding
feature reports the capability error and publishes the effective configuration with
the feature still disabled. Fixing the host does not refresh the snapshot: restart
resman to discover the repaired interface. By contrast, a feature that was merely
disabled in the startup configuration can be enabled by reload without a restart when
its interface was already found usable; failure to enable its controller in an
existing shared cgroup is reported and leaves the feature disabled.
.PP
The published RPM is built with CGO on Enterprise Linux 8 and targets the EL8
userspace ABI (glibc 2.28). It is intended for EL8 and later compatible
Enterprise Linux releases. The RPM is not statically linked because NSS, LDAP,
NIS, and SSSD username resolution require CGO and the system C library.
.PP
PSI event\-driven operation additionally requires kernel PSI support. On
Enterprise Linux systems where PSI is disabled at boot, enable it with
.BR psi=1
as described in the PSI section of this manual.
.SH SYSTEMD RESTART CONTRACT
The packaged systemd unit distinguishes failures that require operator action from
failures that may be transient. Invalid configuration, unusable cgroup capabilities
required by an enabled feature, and invalid MCP TLS credentials exit with status
.BR 78 .
The unit uses the systemd notification protocol and reports readiness only after
configuration, capability, and configured listener initialization have completed.
Consequently,
.B systemctl start resman
waits for that boundary and fails when startup is rejected instead of reporting an
optimistic success for a process that immediately exits. The unit reports the service
as failed and does not restart it automatically. Inspect
.BR "systemctl status resman"
and
.BR "journalctl -u resman" ,
correct the configuration or host capability, then start the service again.
.PP
An error reading or writing setup state for an otherwise present cgroup capability,
including a failed write to
.BR cgroup.subtree_control ,
is treated as potentially transient rather than as proof that the capability is
structurally absent. These and other non-zero exits are retried after 10 seconds. The
unit permits at most three
starts within 60 seconds; after that it remains failed with a start-limit result rather
than retrying forever. After correcting the transient cause, run
.B systemctl reset-failed resman
before starting the service when the start limit was reached. A clean stop is not
automatically restarted.
.SH ARCHITECTURE AND CPU ALLOCATION
The system normalizes current online CPU capacity to 1000 CPU Points and uses a
three-level cgroups v2 hierarchy:
.IP 1. 3
Keeps
.B CPU_RESERVE_POINTS
outside the finite parent; the parent pool is 1000 minus the reserve
.IP 2.
Creates
.I limited/guaranteed
and
.I limited/best_effort
internal domains below that parent
.IP 3.
Places mapped eligible users in the guaranteed domain with their exact map weight;
unmapped eligible users receive equal leaf weights inside the aggregate
.B CPU_BEST_EFFORT_POINTS
domain
.IP 4.
Keeps every user leaf unlimited by
.BR cpu.max ;
only the parent has a finite quota
.PP
The reserve is nominal headroom outside the ResMan parent, not exclusive physical
CPU reservation against arbitrary host workloads. A zero reserve still programs a
finite full-capacity
.B cpu.max
on the parent. CFS may throttle that parent and deliver less than its nominal quota
under saturation. CPU Points guarantees are relative shares of the bandwidth actually
delivered to the parent, not absolute host CPU floors or cpuset isolation. They apply
only while CPU enforcement is active and only to processes successfully acquired by
ResMan; process exclusions and PID-namespace rejections reduce coverage.
.PP
The map named by
.B CPU_POINTS_FILE
must begin with
.B [resman-cpu-points-map-v1]
and then contain exact
.I username=points
assignments. The marker must be the first physical line. UTF-8 with LF or CRLF is
accepted without a BOM; empty lines and unindented full-line comments are allowed;
an assignment has exactly one equals sign, no surrounding whitespace, and canonical
decimal points from 1 through 1000. The complete username is passed byte-for-byte to
NSS. Thus
.B john.smith=200
names the exact dotted identity rather than a nested key. Duplicate names, duplicate
resolved UIDs, ambiguous or non-exact NSS results, unsafe ancestors, symbolic links,
non-regular files, wrong ownership, or a mode other than 0600 reject the complete
snapshot. The packaged default is the root-owned regular file
.B /etc/resman/cpu-points.map
below root-owned mode-0700
.BR /etc/resman .
Configured guarantees plus the best-effort entitlement must not exceed the pool.
Resource division occurs proportionally through cgroup
.B cpu.weight
values. Unused mapped capacity is first borrowed by runnable mapped siblings;
best effort borrows it only when the whole guaranteed domain is inactive. The active
guarantee total and guaranteed-domain weight follow acquired/applied leaves, not
instantaneous scheduler runnability. For example, with pool 900, mapped guarantees
300 and 300, and best effort 100, either runnable mapped user can borrow its mapped
sibling's idle capacity; best effort remains at its class share until both mapped
leaves are idle, then it can use the parent.
.PP
Changes to the reserve, best-effort entitlement, or map contents are applied as
one composite hot-reload epoch. Both files are confirmed before and after kernel
reconciliation. Changing
.B CPU_POINTS_FILE
itself requires a restart; a rejected path change never redirects the live reload.
An active user's guaranteed/best-effort class cannot change until that user is
released, while same-class weight changes and inactive membership changes are dynamic.
The rejection is atomic and creates no pending class state. Wait for normal release or
first make the UID CPU-ineligible and reload, confirm that it is released, change the
map and reload, then restore eligibility with a later reload.
.PP
Practical example with four online CPUs, reserve 250, guarantees 300 and 300,
and best effort 100:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The finite parent receives 750 points, or three nominal CPUs
.IP \(bu
The two mapped users have weight 300 each
.IP \(bu
All unmapped eligible users share the best-effort domain weight 100
.RE
.PP
For a saturated observation window, use synchronized
.B cpu.stat
deltas. The guaranteed-domain ratio is
.B guaranteed_domain_usage_delta / parent_usage_delta
and should follow
.BR active_guarantee_points / (active_guarantee_points + best_effort_points) .
A mapped leaf follows
.BR user_points / (active_guarantee_points + best_effort_points) .
Use at least a 60-second window. The functional gate compares against a measured
same-host reference with tolerances of 0.5 percentage points for the domain and 1.0
point for a leaf rather than assuming the algebraic target is delivered exactly.
Positive parent throttling and nominal under-delivery are expected under saturation.
.PP
CPU limit eligibility is controlled by
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST
and
.BR USER_EXCLUDE_LIST .
An empty or unset
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST
keeps metrics monitoring active but makes no user eligible for CPU limits.
Set
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST=.*
to make every non\-excluded user eligible. This fail\-safe CPU behavior does
not change the independent RAM and I/O include lists, where an empty list
continues to include all users.
.SH RAM LIMITS
When enabled, the daemon monitors total memory usage by non\-system users and applies limits when the configured threshold is exceeded.
.PP
RAM limiting uses cgroups v2 memory controller with two levels of control:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B memory.max
- hard accounting boundary at which the kernel attempts reclaim and may invoke cgroup OOM
.IP \(bu
.B memory.high
- soft limit (memory pressure triggers reclaim, processes may throttle)
.RE
.PP
Configuration parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
.B RAM_LIMIT_ENABLED
- Enable RAM limiting (true/false)
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_THRESHOLD
- Activation threshold percentage of total user RAM usage
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_RELEASE_THRESHOLD
- Deactivation threshold percentage
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_QUOTA_PER_USER
- Per\-user RAM limit (when limits active)
.IP \(bu
.B DISABLE_SWAP
- Set memory.swap.max=0 to prevent swap usage (true/false)
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_HIGH_RATIO
- Ratio between memory.high and memory.max (0.0\-1.0, default 0.8)
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_USER_INCLUDE_LIST
- Regex patterns for users subject to RAM limits (empty = all)
.IP \(bu
.B RAM_USER_EXCLUDE_LIST
- Regex patterns for users excluded from RAM limits
.PP
When RAM limits are active, each user's cgroup receives both
.B memory.max
and
.B memory.high
limits according to the configured quotas and ratio.
.PP
Moving a running process does not transfer its existing memory charges. Dynamic first
ingress therefore constrains post-ingress charges only: process-derived UID memory
remains complete while cgroup RAM coverage is reported as partial. ResMan refuses CPU
activation from a standalone RAM cgroup and defers a CPU release that would cross a
parent while RAM enforcement is active. Release or disable RAM enforcement, allow
reconciliation to complete, and retry the CPU transition. I/O-only transitions remain
permitted because the logical block-I/O ledger preserves counter continuity.
.PP
The normal composition places
.B memory.high
below
.BR memory.max .
Without swap or reclaimable pages, a process can remain alive but make negligible
progress at high indefinitely: the memory-current value plateaus below max, high
events continue rising, and max, OOM, OOM-kill and OOM-group-kill counters remain zero.
Requested allocation size alone therefore does not promise termination. Raise or
disable high, provide reclaimable capacity or swap, or release the RAM limit. An
explicit high-equals-max control is a distinct diagnostic configuration that can
exercise max/OOM behavior without the lower high boundary stalling progress first.
.PP
A user eligible for RAM or I/O but not CPU limiting is moved to a standalone
per\-user cgroup with
.BR cpu.max="max 100000" .
It does not inherit the finite CPU Points parent quota. Consequently,
the CPU Points parent cannot suppress RAM\-only or I/O\-only limits.
.SH IO LIMITS
When enabled, the daemon monitors I/O usage and applies bandwidth and IOPS limits via cgroups v2 io controller.
.PP
IO limiting supports both bandwidth (bytes per second) and IOPS (operations per second) limits for read and write operations independently.
.PP
Configuration parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
.B IO_LIMIT_ENABLED
- Enable IO limiting (true/false)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_THRESHOLD
- Activation percentage applied independently to each configured I/O dimension;
  any dimension at or above the threshold activates or maintains enforcement
.IP \(bu
.B IO_RELEASE_THRESHOLD
- Release percentage; every configured I/O dimension must be below it
.IP \(bu
.B IO_READ_BPS
- Read bandwidth limit (e.g., "100M", "1G")
.IP \(bu
.B IO_WRITE_BPS
- Write bandwidth limit
.IP \(bu
.B IO_READ_IOPS
- Read IOPS limit (0 = unlimited)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_WRITE_IOPS
- Write IOPS limit (0 = unlimited)
.PP
Bandwidth values of "max" or an empty value and IOPS values of 0 disable only
their own dimension. Bandwidth decisions use per-user /proc/PID/io byte rates.
When either IOPS dimension is configured, ResMan places every I/O-eligible
user's enforceable processes in an unlimited per-user observation cgroup and
derives read/write IOPS from io.stat rios/wios. Page-cache hits, pipe traffic,
and socket traffic therefore cannot trigger device-IOPS enforcement. The same
logical counters continue across moves into and out of the shared CPU hierarchy.
The first sample after observation begins or its process policy changes is
unknown rather than zero. Available users remain a lower bound that may prove
activation, but one eligible user without a baseline blocks the global release
decision. Continuous eligible-user churn can therefore prolong active I/O
limits. The activation rule is any-of across configured dimensions and the
release rule is all-of.
.PP
An unavailable /proc/PID/io sample for an enforceable I/O-eligible process is
unknown, not zero. Available rates remain a lower bound and may still prove an
activation threshold exceeded. Otherwise resman maintains the current state;
active limits are never released from an incomplete I/O sample. Current
coverage failures are exported through
.BR resman_procfs_unavailable_processes {access}.
.IP \(bu
.B IO_DEVICE_FILTER
- Device specification. "all" enumerates all whole block devices in /sys/block;
  alternatively specify one "major:minor" device.
.IP \(bu
.B IO_THRESHOLD_DURATION
- Seconds to wait before activating IO limits (0 = immediate)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_USER_INCLUDE_LIST
- Regex patterns for users subject to IO limits (empty = all)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_USER_EXCLUDE_LIST
- Regex patterns for users excluded from IO limits
.PP
IO starvation auto\-remediation can be enabled to temporarily boost limits when processes experience excessive throttling:
.PP
Remediation applies only to users that currently have active resman limits and pass
.B IO_USER_INCLUDE_LIST
and
.B IO_USER_EXCLUDE_LIST
The configured I/O limits are multiplied for the boost duration and restored exactly when the boost expires. Expiration is enforced regardless of the current PSI value; with
.B IO_REVERT_ON_NORMAL=true
the original limits are restored earlier when pressure returns to normal.
.IP \(bu 2
.B IO_REMEDIATION_ENABLED
- Enable auto\-remediation (true/false)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_STARVATION_THRESHOLD
- Seconds of continuous throttling before remediation
.IP \(bu
.B IO_BOOST_MULTIPLIER
- Multiplier for temporary limits (e.g., 2.0 = double)
.IP \(bu
.B IO_BOOST_DURATION
- Duration of boost in seconds
.IP \(bu
.B IO_BOOST_MAX_PER_HOUR
- Maximum boosts per user per hour
.IP \(bu
.B IO_PSI_THRESHOLD
- PSI some avg10 % threshold for detection
.IP \(bu
.B IO_REVERT_ON_NORMAL
- Revert limits when I/O returns to normal (true/false)
.PP
.SH PROCESS CGROUP RESTORATION
Before moving a process into the shared limited cgroup, resman persistently
records its original cgroup together with the process start time. At release,
the process is returned to that exact cgroup when it can legally accept
processes, so systemd session and service membership are preserved.
.PP
Processes whose original cgroup no longer exists, and descendants for which no
unambiguous original destination can be resolved, are moved to
.I CGROUP_BASE/recovery/user_UID
instead of the cgroup v2 root. This recovery hierarchy is owned by resman and is
preserved while it contains live processes.
.PP
An existing origin that distributes controllers to child cgroups is an internal
cgroup v2 node and cannot accept processes. Resman treats it like an unavailable
origin and restores the process to the recovery leaf. The PID start time is
revalidated immediately before each restore write. If any process cannot be
restored safely during shutdown, the daemon exits non-zero and the service stop
is not reported as successful.
.PP
Recovery leaves use the internal unlimited value
.BR cpu.max="max 100000" .
Resman never writes a recovery quota into cgroups managed by systemd.
.PP
Live policy reconciliation remains fail-closed. If an excluded process has no
same-start-time recorded or inherited origin, it remains constrained, the cycle
reports an error, and the later history, I/O remediation, workload detection,
and PSI-reversion stages still run. Prometheus reports this persistent condition
through
.B resman_errors_total
with
.B component="process_membership"
and
.BR error_type="origin_unavailable" ;
other reconciliation failures use
.BR error_type="reconciliation_failure" .
.PP
To clear an unavailable-origin condition, stop or restart the affected process
under its owning service or session. If that cannot be done while resman is
running, stop resman cleanly so shutdown can place the process in its recovery
leaf, then restart the owning service or session before starting resman again.
.PP
.SH PSI EVENT-DRIVEN MODE
When enabled, the daemon uses Linux Pressure Stall Information (PSI) files to
trigger control cycles when system pressure exceeds configured thresholds. The
fixed polling interval remains a fallback heartbeat.
.PP
PSI is not another CPU usage percentage. It measures stall/pressure: time in
which runnable tasks or IO operations are waiting because resources are
contended. For example, on a 4-core host, 4 CPU-bound threads can show 100% CPU
with little PSI if there is no meaningful run queue; more runnable threads than
available cores are more likely to generate CPU PSI.
.PP
The daemon opens
.B cpu.pressure
and
.B io.pressure
files from the cgroup root and uses the
.B poll()
syscall with
.B POLLPRI
to block until the kernel signals that pressure has exceeded the stall threshold.
Older CPU pressure files that expose only the
.B some
line are supported; the
.B full
line is optional.
When a PSI event fires, the daemon immediately runs a control cycle.
Control-cycle logs and Prometheus metrics include the trigger source, such as
.B initial ,
.B ticker ,
.B psi_system_cpu ,
.B psi_system_io ,
.B psi_user_cpu ,
or
.B psi_user_io .
.PP
A fallback polling interval (heartbeat) ensures control cycles still run if PSI
events are not generated for an extended period (e.g., when the system is idle).
This heartbeat is for the decision loop only. Prometheus/Grafana metrics are
refreshed separately using
.B METRICS_REFRESH_INTERVAL
so dashboards remain current even when no PSI event fires.
.PP
When a limited user experiences CPU throttling (pressure exceeds threshold in their
per-user cgroup), the daemon temporarily boosts their cpu.weight to grant more CPU
time within the shared quota (adaptive fairness). The boost reverts automatically
after the configured duration.
.PP
Configuration parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
.B PSI_EVENT_DRIVEN
- Enable PSI event-driven mode (true/false, default: false)
.PP
PSI mode, CPU and I/O stall thresholds, the tracking window, fallback interval,
and metrics refresh interval support configuration reload. Changes to the mode,
thresholds, or window rebuild the kernel PSI monitors. Loop interval changes
take effect immediately.
.IP \(bu
.B PSI_CPU_STALL_THRESHOLD
- CPU stall threshold in microseconds (default: 50000 = 5% on 1s window)
.IP \(bu
.B PSI_IO_STALL_THRESHOLD
- IO stall threshold in microseconds (default: 50000)
.IP \(bu
.B PSI_WINDOW_US
- PSI tracking window in microseconds (default: 1000000 = 1 second)
.IP \(bu
.B PSI_FALLBACK_INTERVAL
- Fallback polling interval in seconds when event-driven (default: 300 = 5min)
.IP \(bu
.B METRICS_REFRESH_INTERVAL
- Prometheus/Grafana refresh interval in seconds when event-driven (default: 30)
.PP
Requirements:
.IP \(bu 2
Linux kernel >= 4.20 with CONFIG_PSI=y
.IP \(bu
cgroups v2 with cpu.pressure and io.pressure files available
.IP \(bu
.B golang.org/x/sys
package (included as direct dependency)
.PP
Example configuration:
.sp
.nf
PSI_EVENT_DRIVEN=true
PSI_CPU_STALL_THRESHOLD=50000    # 5% CPU pressure
PSI_IO_STALL_THRESHOLD=50000     # 5% IO pressure
PSI_WINDOW_US=1000000            # 1 second window
PSI_FALLBACK_INTERVAL=300        # 5 minute heartbeat
METRICS_REFRESH_INTERVAL=30      # Dashboard refresh interval
.fi
.sp
.SH WORKLOAD PATTERN DETECTION
When enabled, the daemon analyzes historical CPU usage to classify eligible users
as "batch" or "interactive" and selects the corresponding RAM policy. CPU capacity
remains owned exclusively by CPU Points.
.PP
Pattern detection respects
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST
and
.B USER_EXCLUDE_LIST
and never applies policy to an excluded user. Pattern RAM limits are applied to the
user's existing sub\-cgroup only while global limits are active; pattern analysis
does not move processes or change CPU weights.
.PP
When a classification becomes unknown, the user becomes ineligible, or
.B AUTODETECT_PATTERNS
is disabled, any active pattern policy is removed. A limited user retains its CPU
Points allocation and returns to the configured non-pattern RAM limit.
.PP
The pattern detection algorithm uses a sliding window of
.B PATTERN_HISTORY_HOURS
hours and requires at least
.B PATTERN_MIN_SAMPLES
distinct hourly buckets before making decisions. Classification confidence must exceed
.B PATTERN_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD
(0.0\-1.0).
.PP
Samples collected within the same wall\-clock hour are aggregated into one hourly bucket. Each hourly bucket has equal weight regardless of polling frequency or PSI events. Buckets older than
.B PATTERN_HISTORY_HOURS
are removed, including for continuously active users.
.PP
Detected patterns influence resource allocation:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B Batch night
- Users with high resource usage during night hours receive the RAM quota (
.B BATCH_NIGHT_RAM_QUOTA
).
.IP \(bu
.B Interactive day
- Users with moderate daytime usage receive the RAM quota (
.B INTERACTIVE_RAM_QUOTA
).
.RE
.PP
Configuration parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
.B AUTODETECT_PATTERNS
- Enable automatic pattern detection (true/false)
.IP \(bu
.B PATTERN_HISTORY_HOURS
- Historical window in hours (default: 168 = 1 week)
.IP \(bu
.B PATTERN_MIN_SAMPLES
- Minimum number of distinct hourly buckets required for classification
.IP \(bu
.B PATTERN_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD
- Confidence threshold (0.0\-1.0)
.IP \(bu
.B BATCH_NIGHT_RAM_QUOTA
- RAM quota for batch users (bytes or case\-insensitive K/M/G/T suffix)
.IP \(bu
.B INTERACTIVE_RAM_QUOTA
- RAM quota for interactive users (bytes or case\-insensitive K/M/G/T suffix)
.PP
Pattern detection runs hourly and updates user classifications automatically.
.SH CONTROL CYCLE
The daemon executes control cycles at regular intervals:
.IP 1. 3
Collects system metrics (total CPU usage, per\-user CPU, load average)
.IP 2.
Decision making based on configured thresholds
.IP 3.
Applying/removing limits via cgroups
.IP 4.
Logging and Prometheus metrics update (if enabled)
.SH PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATIONS
The daemon incorporates several performance optimizations to minimize overhead on managed systems:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B gopsutil integration
- Efficient process scanning via the proven gopsutil library, replacing custom /proc parsing.
.IP \(bu
.B sync.Map regex cache
- Thread\-safe caching of compiled regular expressions for user filtering, eliminating race conditions.
.IP \(bu
.B Batch PID moves
- Grouping process ID migrations during cgroup cleanup to reduce system call overhead.
.IP \(bu
.B Consolidated /proc scans
- Single scan of /proc for all user metrics, reducing I/O operations by ~70%.
.IP \(bu
.B Sharded mutexes
- Fine\-grained locking for per\-process CPU tracking to minimize contention.
.IP \(bu
.B Pre\-allocated slices
- Estimated capacity allocation for dynamic slices to reduce heap allocations.
.IP \(bu
.B Per-entry cache retention
- Each metric value retains its owning TTL, while completed process scans prune exited PID baselines.
.RE
.PP
These optimizations ensure that monitoring overhead remains low even on systems with thousands of processes.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
The configuration file supports key=value format. Full\-line comments start with
\(aq#\(aq. An inline \(aq#\(aq starts a comment only outside quotes and when preceded by
whitespace.
Timeout parameters for cgroup operations, retry delays, and MCP shutdown can be configured to improve reliability in high\-load scenarios.
.PP
.B METRICS_CACHE_TTL
controls value reuse and must be at least one second. A value remains valid at the exact TTL boundary and expires
after it; periodic cleanup does not shorten values above five minutes. CPU and I/O
process baselines are independent of this TTL and remain valid for active PID and
start-time identities until a completed scan proves that the process disappeared.
.PP
The active configuration path is selected by the
.B \-\-config
command\-line option (default:
.IR /etc/resman/resman.conf ).
It cannot be redirected by a key inside the configuration file or by an
environment variable.
.PP
When the default path is selected, startup refuses any legacy
.I /etc/resman.conf
or RPM-saved
.I /etc/resman.conf.rpmsave
and the secret-bearing
.I /etc/resman.conf.backup
and
.I /etc/resman.conf.tmp
entries, plus every potential configuration copy matching
.IR /etc/resman.conf.backup_* ,
even when the new packaged configuration already exists. Stop resman,
choose the authoritative authored contents from the legacy or RPM-saved file,
install them as a regular file at
.IR /etc/resman/resman.conf ,
remove the legacy source files, move any needed operator-managed matching copies
to a protected archive outside the legacy path, securely remove generated or
unneeded matching copies and fixed-name orphaned artifacts, and restart. The
read-only startup guard deliberately treats every matching name as potentially
credential-bearing; automatic cleanup removes only exact historical generated
names. An explicitly selected custom configuration path is not treated as a
legacy fallback.
.PP
When metrics persistence is enabled at its default
.I /var/lib/resman/metrics.db
path, startup likewise refuses a legacy
.IR /etc/resman/metrics.db .
Earlier database schemas are incompatible: archive or delete the database
and restart so resman can create schema version 4. It is never migrated or opened
under the new path.
.PP
Configuration updates initiated through MCP require this path to be a regular file.
Symbolic links, including dangling links, are rejected without replacing the link or
changing its target. Select the target itself with
.B \-\-config
or replace the link with a regular file. Atomic replacement preserves the source mode
and ownership and fails before writing secret-bearing content if the service account
cannot apply the required owner. In that case, grant the service permission to change
file ownership or change the source ownership to the service account before retrying.
If a failed atomic replacement cannot restore the rolling backup, stop resman, restore
.IR /etc/resman/resman.conf.backup ,
and restart before accepting another configuration write; the active file and runtime
configuration may differ until recovery is complete. Resman rejects subsequent
configuration persistence attempts until restart. If a newly created configuration has
no rolling backup and its compensating removal fails, stop resman, remove the new file,
and restart.
.PP
.B Authoritative configuration reference
.PP
The complete public-key inventory is generated from the runtime configuration,
defaults, and lifecycle table. It is installed as
.IR /usr/share/doc/resman/CONFIGURATION.md .
.PP
.B Upgrade from 1.25.x through 1.30.8 to 1.31.0
.PP
ResMan 1.31.0 intentionally breaks filesystem, database,
configuration, MCP, Prometheus, hook, capability and container contracts. Before
installing it, follow
.IR /usr/share/doc/resman/UPGRADING.md .
It lists the visible symptom, cause and required action for every known
discontinuity; old schemas, keys and wire aliases are not preserved.
.PP
The copyable configuration remains
.IR /etc/resman/resman.conf .
.PP
The default Prometheus bind is
.IR 127.0.0.1 .
A bind to
.I 0.0.0.0
is a non-default remote exposure and requires TLS, authentication, and firewall
restrictions.
.B SYSTEM_UID_MAX
is normally derived from
.I /proc/sys/kernel/pid_max
at startup, with 60000 used only if that file cannot be read. An empty
.B USER_INCLUDE_LIST
makes nobody eligible for CPU limiting, while empty RAM and I/O include lists
select every non-excluded user.
.PP
Idle user release is based on the per-user CPU exponential moving average.
.B MIN_ACTIVE_TIME
protects the most recent active enforcement epoch across CPU and RAM or IO,
because global deactivation releases every resource together. It also acts as
the minimum hold time after an individual CPU user is first limited or re-added.
Re-adding a user does not reset the CPU enforcement epoch. A future per-resource
release model must define and test its own per-resource activation timestamps.
Dynamic RAM and IO enable or user-filter changes are reconciled against already
active user cgroups.
.PP
Database retention runs at startup and every 24 hours. File-backed databases
use incremental auto-vacuum so periodic cleanup does not run a blocking full
database vacuum. Existing databases using auto-vacuum mode NONE are migrated
once at startup; this first upgraded startup runs a full VACUUM and may require
additional time and temporary free disk space proportional to the database
size. Each collection cycle is committed as one transaction.
File-backed databases use WAL mode with a 5-second busy timeout, and timestamps
are stored and queried in UTC.
.PP
Schema version 4 commits one common sample epoch and interval boundary for every
system and user row. CPU Points history separates configured class and optional
guarantee from applied class and weight, raw cgroup diagnostics, nullable comparable
usage deltas, the online-CPU denominator, programmed parent quota, domain use and
parent throttling. RAM history separately reports process-derived memory,
memory.current, applied state, complete or partial charge coverage, memory.high/max
limits, swap policy, and high/max/OOM/kill event deltas. A NULL counter delta means
that no comparable baseline exists after startup, a read failure, counter reset or
cgroup recreation; zero means a comparable counter did not increase. A released
state is emitted only for a user observed in that sample.
.PP
For a file-backed
.B METRICS_DB_PATH
the immediate parent must be a real directory owned by the ResMan process UID
with mode 0700. ResMan creates a missing parent with that mode, but refuses an
existing directory with different ownership or permissions. A pre-existing
database and its
.B -wal
and
.B -shm
sidecars must be regular, non-symbolic-link files owned by the same UID with
mode 0600. Correct ownership and permissions explicitly before restarting;
ResMan does not silently change operator-owned artifacts. The
.B METRICS_DB_PATH
ancestor chain must contain no symbolic links and no directory from which an
untrusted owner, group, or other user can replace the checked child. A sticky
temporary directory is accepted when it protects the process-owned child. The
.B :memory:
database is exempt. These checks occur before SQLite opens the path so a
permissive service umask cannot expose history files.
.nf

# PSI EVENT-DRIVEN MODE (Linux >= 4.20, CONFIG_PSI=y)
# Uses poll() on cpu.pressure/io.pressure to trigger control cycles
# only when system pressure exceeds thresholds. Reduces overhead
# when the system is idle by using a longer fallback interval.
# PSI_EVENT_DRIVEN=true
# PSI_CPU_STALL_THRESHOLD=50000    # Stall threshold in microseconds
# PSI_IO_STALL_THRESHOLD=50000     # IO stall threshold in microseconds
# PSI_WINDOW_US=1000000            # Tracking window (1 second)
# PSI_FALLBACK_INTERVAL=300        # Heartbeat interval (5 minutes)
# METRICS_REFRESH_INTERVAL=30      # Dashboard refresh interval

# MCP TOOLS FOR HISTORICAL METRICS (when METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true):
# - get_user_history: Historical CPU/RAM for a user
# - get_system_history: Historical system metrics
# - get_user_summary: Aggregated statistics (avg/min/max)
# - get_metrics_database_info: Database status and info
.sp
.fi
.PP
A complete example configuration file with all available parameters can be found in the distribution as
.I config/resman.conf.example
.
.SS Activation Timing
.B CPU_THRESHOLD_DURATION
is not measured from process start. resman starts that timer only after a
control cycle measures per-user CPU above
.B CPU_THRESHOLD.
For a newly started CPU-bound process, the effective activation time is approximately:
.PP
.RS
.nf
one CPU delta sample + CPU_THRESHOLD_DURATION
.fi
.RE
.PP
With
.B POLLING_INTERVAL=30,
.B CPU_THRESHOLD_DURATION=90,
limits normally activate about 120 seconds after the first observation, plus
polling alignment. Every process state is included in instantaneous CPU deltas.
.B PROCESS_MIN_AGE_SECONDS
only stabilizes the lifetime-average CPU metric and does not delay activation.
.PP
With
.B IGNORE_SYSTEM_LOAD=false,
a high load average suppresses activation only when CPU-eligible users account
for less than half of measured aggregate host CPU activity. Host CPU is converted
from normalized 0-100 percentage into aggregate per-core percentage before the
comparison. An exact half remains actionable. If the host CPU sample is unavailable,
activation is delayed without discarding threshold-duration progress or claiming an
unmeasured external cause.
.PP
Global deactivation requires every actively limited user's CPU EMA to remain below
.B CPU_RELEASE_THRESHOLD
for three
.B POLLING_INTERVAL
periods. This cool-down uses elapsed wall-clock time, so additional control
cycles triggered by PSI events cannot shorten it.
.PP
.B total_cpu_usage
is the host-wide normalized CPU percentage (0-100). Activation decisions use
per-user CPU usage
.RB ( cpu_eligible_users_cpu_usage ),
which is the sum of process CPU and can exceed 100 on multi-core systems.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
All configuration options can be overridden by environment variables
with the same name. Example:
.sp
.nf
LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG resman \-\-config /etc/resman/resman.conf
.fi
.SH LOG FILES
.I /var/log/resman.log
.br
The log file is automatically rotated when it reaches the maximum size
configured in
.B LOG_MAX_SIZE,
which must be greater than zero.
Rotation creates a backup file with .1 extension.
New log files are created mode 0600. When an existing regular log is opened,
ResMan preserves only its owner/group read-write bits and removes all execute bits
and all permissions for other users. The rotated backup and replacement inherit
that sanitized access mode and ownership. The managed path must be a regular file;
symbolic links are rejected without changing their target. Packaged rsyslog and
logrotate defaults use 0600.
.PP
File and syslog writes, rotation steps, and sink close operations are checked. If the
configured sink fails, ResMan writes a bounded diagnostic directly to standard error
without recursively calling the failed logger. The failed record itself is not copied
to the fallback because it can contain sensitive context.
A later successful write marks the primary sink healthy again while retaining
cumulative and last-failure diagnostics. A control cycle whose completion record
cannot be written finishes its protective work and returns a degraded result instead
of claiming a successful cycle log. Ordinary diagnostic call sites use the same
health and fallback contract without changing their owning operation's result.
.SH STATE FILES
.I /run/resman-cgroups.txt
.br
Tracks created cgroups to enable cleanup after restart.
.br
.I /run/resman-cgroups-origins.json
.br
Stores the original cgroup, process start time, parent, and session identity
needed to restore processes safely after limits are removed or the daemon
restarts. The file is replaced atomically and is readable only by its owner.
.SH PROMETHEUS METRICS
When enabled, the daemon exposes metrics at the configured
.B /metrics
endpoint over HTTP or HTTPS.
.IP \(bu 2
resman_cpu_total_usage_percent \- Total CPU usage
.IP \(bu
resman_all_users_cpu_usage_percent \- Non\-system user CPU usage
.IP \(bu
resman_memory_usage_megabytes \- Total system memory usage
.IP \(bu
resman_all_users_count \- Number of active non\-system users
.IP \(bu
resman_cpu_eligible_users_count \- Users eligible under CPU policy
.IP \(bu
resman_ram_eligible_users_count \- Users eligible under RAM policy
.IP \(bu
resman_io_eligible_users_count \- Users eligible under I/O policy
.IP \(bu
resman_cpu_actively_limited_users_count \- Users with observed CPU limits
.IP \(bu
resman_actively_limited_users_count \- Distinct users with observed CPU, RAM, or I/O limits
.IP \(bu
resman_cpu_limits_active \- CPU limit status (1=active, 0=inactive)
.IP \(bu
resman_resource_limits_active \- RAM or I/O limit status (1=active, 0=inactive)
.IP \(bu
resman_any_limits_active \- Any CPU, RAM, or I/O limit status (1=active, 0=inactive)
.IP \(bu
resman_system_load_average \- System load average (1 minute)
.IP \(bu
resman_user_cpu_usage_percent{uid, username} \- CPU usage per user
.IP \(bu
resman_user_cpu_usage_average_percent{uid, username} \- Average CPU usage per user
.IP \(bu
resman_user_cpu_usage_ema_percent{uid, username} \- Exponential moving average of CPU usage per user
.IP \(bu
resman_user_memory_usage_bytes{uid, username} \- Memory usage per user (PSS in bytes, RSS fallback)
.IP \(bu
resman_user_io_read_bytes_total{uid, username} \- Bytes read from block devices per user (when IO limits enabled)
.IP \(bu
resman_user_io_write_bytes_total{uid, username} \- Bytes written to block devices per user
.IP \(bu
resman_user_io_read_ops_total{uid, username} \- Read-family syscall count from /proc/PID/io syscr per user; this is not block-device IOPS
.IP \(bu
resman_user_io_write_ops_total{uid, username} \- Write-family syscall count from /proc/PID/io syscw per user; this is not block-device IOPS
.IP \(bu
resman_user_process_count{uid, username} \- Number of processes per user
.IP \(bu
resman_user_cpu_limit_active{uid, username} \- User limit status (1=limited, 0=unlimited)
.IP \(bu
resman_cpu_points_parent_usage_microseconds_delta \- Effective parent CPU time delivered during the common control-cycle interval; the allocation denominator
.IP \(bu
resman_cpu_points_guaranteed_domain_usage_microseconds_delta and resman_cpu_points_best_effort_domain_usage_microseconds_delta \- Synchronized class-domain CPU time
.IP \(bu
resman_cpu_points_parent_throttled_periods_delta and resman_cpu_points_parent_throttled_microseconds_delta \- CFS throttling in the common interval; positive values can be expected under saturation
.IP \(bu
resman_config_reload_state{state} \- One-hot terminal reload state: never, applied, refused, or failed; refused means the disk candidate is pending while the prior epoch remains active
.IP \(bu
resman_config_reload_last_attempt_timestamp_seconds and resman_config_reload_last_success_timestamp_seconds \- Timestamps that distinguish the latest terminal attempt from the latest acknowledged epoch
.IP \(bu
resman_user_cpu_points_configured_guarantee{uid, username} \- Optional mapped-user guarantee; absent for best-effort users
.IP \(bu
resman_user_cpu_points_applied_weight{uid, username} \- Raw verified leaf cpu.weight, not delivered CPU Points
.IP \(bu
resman_user_cpu_points_process_coverage{uid, username, coverage} \- none, complete, partial, or unavailable host-process coverage
.IP \(bu
resman_user_ram_cgroup_memory_current_bytes{uid, username} \- Post-ingress cgroup memory.current accounting, not complete process-derived UID memory
.PP
All per-user series are published exclusively from the authoritative control-cycle
sample. Observation-only refreshes update system-wide telemetry but do not write or
remove per-user series, so CPU alerts and the exported EMA use the same sampling
stream as enforcement.
CPU Points usage and throttling deltas share one explicit interval. Compare domain or
leaf delivery with effective parent usage, never with the nominal pool or programmed
quota. CFS can under-deliver nominal quota. Idle mapped capacity serves runnable
guaranteed siblings first; best effort borrows only when the complete guaranteed
domain is inactive.
.IP \(bu
resman_cpu_limits_activated_total \- Confirmed inactive-to-active limit transitions (counter)
.IP \(bu
resman_cpu_limits_deactivated_total \- Confirmed active-to-inactive limit transitions (counter)
.IP \(bu
resman_control_cycle_triggers_total{trigger} \- Control cycles by trigger source
.IP \(bu
resman_psi_events_total{type, scope} \- PSI pressure events received from the kernel
.IP \(bu
resman_psi_last_event_timestamp_seconds{type, scope} \- Unix timestamp of last PSI event
.IP \(bu
resman_control_cycle_duration_seconds \- Complete control cycle duration, including failed and suspended cycles (histogram)
.IP \(bu
resman_metrics_collection_duration_seconds \- System metrics collection duration for control cycles and metrics-only refreshes (histogram)
.IP \(bu
resman_errors_total{component, error_type} \- Operational errors by component and bounded error type (counter)
.IP \(bu
resman_limit_hook_executions_total{hook_type, outcome} \- Terminal script and HTTP hook outcomes; outcome is success, failure, timeout, or cancelled (counter)
.IP \(bu
resman_cgroup_ingress_skipped_total{reason} \- Processes kept outside ResMan-owned cgroups because their PID namespace mismatched or was unavailable (counter)
.IP \(bu
resman_procfs_unavailable_processes{access} \- Current processes missing executable_identity or io_decision procfs access (gauge)
.PP
All user-specific metrics include
.B uid
and
.B username
labels for easy filtering and aggregation in Grafana dashboards.
The
.B resman_user_cpu_limit_active
gauge reports observed CPU enforcement state: 1 means the user is in the
CPU-limited cgroup, while 0 means CPU enforcement is not observed.
.PP
Example queries:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Sum of all user memory:
.I sum(resman_user_memory_usage_bytes)
.IP \(bu
Top 5 users by CPU:
.I topk(5, resman_user_cpu_usage_percent)
.IP \(bu
Processes per user:
.I resman_user_process_count{username="francesco"}
.IP \(bu
Only limited users:
.I resman_user_cpu_limit_active == 1
.RE
.SH MCP SERVER (MODEL CONTEXT PROTOCOL)
When enabled, the daemon exposes an MCP server for AI assistant integration.
The MCP server provides tools and resources for querying system status and generating reports.
.PP
ResMan accepts only MCP revision 2026-07-28 over stdio or HTTP. The protocol is
stateless: every request is self-describing, HTTP authentication is evaluated
per request, and no MCP session identifier or initialization exchange is
supported. Pre-2026 revisions and compatibility fallbacks are rejected.
.PP
MCP over HTTP is HTTPS-only and requires
.B MCP_TLS_ENABLED=true,
a server certificate, and a key. The default MCP certificate and key paths are
the same literal paths used by Prometheus, but the MCP and Prometheus settings
are independent. The default minimum version is TLS 1.3. Setting
.B MCP_TLS_CA_FILE
enables mutual TLS and makes a client certificate mandatory in addition to the
per-request bearer token. The stdio transport does not require certificates.
.PP
Available MCP tools (this list is verified against the production discovery response):
.\" BEGIN MCP TOOL INVENTORY
.IP \(bu 2
.B get_system_status
\- Current CPU/memory status with hostname
.IP \(bu
.B get_user_metrics
\- Per-user CPU, memory, and process metrics
.IP \(bu
.B get_active_users
\- List of active non-system users
.IP \(bu
.B get_limits_status
\- CPU limits status and details
.IP \(bu
.B get_cgroup_info
\- Cgroup information for a specific user
.IP \(bu
.B get_configuration
\- Current daemon configuration
.IP \(bu
.B get_control_history
\- Recent control cycle history
.IP \(bu
.B get_cpu_report
\- Comprehensive CPU usage report (formatted text)
.IP \(bu
.B get_mem_report
\- Comprehensive memory usage report (formatted text)
.IP \(bu
.B set_user_exclude_list
\- Persist and apply CPU exclusion patterns (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B set_user_include_list
\- Persist and apply CPU eligibility patterns (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_user_filters
\- Get current CPU include and exclude patterns
.IP \(bu
.B validate_user_filter_pattern
\- Validate a user-filter regular expression
.IP \(bu
.B activate_limits
\- Manually activate CPU limits (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B deactivate_limits
- Manually deactivate limits (requires MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_user_history
- Historical CPU/RAM metrics for a specific user (requires METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_system_history
- Historical system metrics (requires METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_user_summary
- Aggregated statistics (avg/min/max) for a user (requires METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true)
.IP \(bu
.B get_metrics_database_info
- Database status and information (requires METRICS_DB_ENABLED=true)
.\" END MCP TOOL INVENTORY
.PP
System-wide payloads and the shared active-user and configuration schemas include a
.B hostname
field for multi-server environment identification. Per-user entries are identified by
uid and username inside those host-scoped responses.
.PP
Fixed MCP resources (this list is verified against the production discovery response):
.\" BEGIN MCP FIXED RESOURCE INVENTORY
.IP \(bu 2
.B resman://system/status
\- Real-time system status
.IP \(bu
.B resman://users/active
\- List of active users
.IP \(bu
.B resman://limits/status
\- Current limits status
.IP \(bu
.B resman://config
\- Current configuration
.\" END MCP FIXED RESOURCE INVENTORY
.PP
MCP resource URI templates (this list is verified against the production discovery response):
.\" BEGIN MCP RESOURCE TEMPLATE INVENTORY
.IP \(bu
.B resman://users/{uid}/metrics
\- Per-user metrics
.IP \(bu
.B resman://cgroups/{uid}
\- Cgroup information
.\" END MCP RESOURCE TEMPLATE INVENTORY
.PP
MCP prompts (this list is verified against the production discovery response):
.\" BEGIN MCP PROMPT INVENTORY
.IP \(bu 2
.B system-health
\- Quick system health check with assessment
.IP \(bu
.B user-analysis
\- Analyze resource usage by user in table format
.IP \(bu
.B troubleshooting
\- Diagnose CPU limit issues
.\" END MCP PROMPT INVENTORY
.PP
The
.B get_active_users
tool and
.B resman://users/active
resource share one object schema with hostname, server_role, and user entries containing
both uid and username. The
.B get_configuration
tool and
.B resman://config
resource share one resource-policy schema covering CPU, RAM, and I/O settings plus host
identity. The payload deliberately excludes credentials and settings outside that scope.
The per-user metrics tool and resource likewise share one typed projection. Historical
user and system records use distinct schemas because their fields have different meanings.
.PP
The
.B get_cgroup_info
tool and the cgroup resource share one underscore-named JSON schema. They pair each CPU
and memory cgroup interface value with an explicit
.B *_available
boolean. If an interface cannot be read, its value is omitted, availability is false,
and
.B *_unavailable_reason
is one of the following bounded values:
.IP \(bu 2
.B not_present
\- Verify that the managed cgroup still exists. If the interface is required by the
enabled configuration, repair or enable the corresponding cpu or memory controller in
the delegated hierarchy and restart ResMan; an interface for a disabled feature may
legitimately be absent.
.IP \(bu
.B permission_denied
\- Restore the documented root/delegation model and cgroup mount permissions; do not
make individual interface files world-readable.
.IP \(bu
.B read_error
\- Inspect the ResMan journal and kernel log, verify that the managed cgroup still
exists, and investigate persistent cgroup-filesystem failures.
.PP
Available values omit the failure reason. The reason contains neither the attempted
interface path nor raw error text; the existing path field still identifies the managed
cgroup. Clients must not treat an empty value as an unlimited limit.
.PP
Example MCP configuration:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Enable MCP:
.I MCP_ENABLED=true
.IP \(bu
Transport mode:
.I MCP_TRANSPORT=stdio
(or
.IR http )
.IP \(bu
HTTP port (for http transport):
.I MCP_HTTP_PORT=1969
.IP \(bu
TLS certificate and key (mandatory for http transport):
.I MCP_TLS_CERT_FILE=/etc/resman/tls/server.crt
and
.I MCP_TLS_KEY_FILE=/etc/resman/tls/server.key
.IP \(bu
Allow write operations:
.I MCP_ALLOW_WRITE_OPS=false
.RE
.SH LIMIT HOOKS
When
.B LIMIT_HOOK_ENABLED
is true, ResMan can run an external script, call a webservice, or both whenever
a user is newly limited.
.PP
Script hooks are configured with
.B LIMIT_HOOK_SCRIPT
and receive the following environment variables:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
RESMAN_LIMIT_UID
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_USERNAME
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_ENFORCEABLE_CPU_USAGE_PERCENT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_ELIGIBLE_USERS_COUNT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_SHARED_CGROUP
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_TIMESTAMP
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_SERVER_ROLE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_CONFIGURED_CLASS
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_CONFIGURED_GUARANTEE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_LIFECYCLE_STATE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_APPLIED_CLASS
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_APPLIED_WEIGHT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_APPLIED_TO_PROCESSES
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_COMPLETE_UID_WORKLOAD_GUARANTEED
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_RECONCILIATION_DEGRADED
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_CPU_POINTS_PROCESS_COVERAGE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_PID_NAMESPACE_MISMATCH_COUNT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_PID_NAMESPACE_UNAVAILABLE_COUNT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_RAM_CGROUP_MEMORY_CURRENT_BYTES
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_RAM_COVERAGE
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_RAM_COVERAGE_INCOMPLETE_PROCESS_COUNT
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_RAM_SWAP_DISABLED
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_HIGH
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_MAX
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_SWAP_MAX
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_HIGH_EVENTS_DELTA
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_MAX_EVENTS_DELTA
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_OOM_EVENTS_DELTA
.IP \(bu
RESMAN_LIMIT_MEMORY_OOM_KILL_EVENTS_DELTA
.RE
.PP
Webservice hooks are configured with
.B LIMIT_HOOK_URL
and receive an HTTP POST with a JSON payload containing uid, username,
enforceable_cpu_usage_percent, cpu_eligible_users_count, shared_cgroup,
timestamp, source, server_role, the typed CPU Points configured/applied lifecycle,
bounded process coverage, PID-namespace rejection counts, and post-ingress RAM cgroup
coverage. RAM limits and interval `memory.high`, `memory.max`, OOM, and OOM-kill deltas
remain distinct; growing high events with zero max/OOM/kill events can describe supported
throttling or stall and does not imply absent enforcement. An absent mapped guarantee is
not zero, and a partial host subset is never reported as the complete UID workload.
.PP
Hook script stdout and stderr are discarded and are never included in returned
errors or daemon logs. Script failures retain a bounded reason such as timeout or
exit status. Webservice failure logs identify only the URL scheme and host (including
an explicit port); user information, path, query values, and fragments are omitted.
.PP
Hook execution is asynchronous and bounded by
.B LIMIT_HOOK_TIMEOUT
seconds. Every script and HTTP execution records one terminal success, failure,
timeout, or cancellation outcome. On shutdown ResMan rejects new hook dispatch,
cancels in-flight hooks, and waits for them to terminate before continuing cleanup.
The control cycle does not wait for ordinary hook completion.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGHUP
\- Reload configuration
.PP
Runtime thresholds, filters, PSI settings, polling intervals, and
.B LOG_LEVEL
are applied dynamically. This includes
.BR USERNAME_CACHE_TTL
regardless of whether the metrics database is enabled, and
.BR METRICS_DB_RETENTION_DAYS .
Cgroup paths, created-cgroup state path, Prometheus listener and security settings,
.BR LOG_FILE ,
.BR LOG_MAX_SIZE ,
.BR USE_SYSLOG ,
metrics database enablement, path and write interval, server role, and MCP
enablement, transport, listener, authentication, and write permissions
require a service restart. Requested changes to static fields are logged and
rejected with an error naming each key; their previous effective values remain
active. Restart the service to apply those keys. Configuration values and
credentials are not included in that error.
.PP
Reload is published as one configuration epoch. Control cycles and metrics
refreshes finish entirely on the old epoch or wait until all runtime consumers
hold the new epoch; they never observe a partial cross-component update.
.br
.B SIGINT, SIGTERM
\- Graceful shutdown
.SH EXAMPLES
Start with custom configuration:
.sp
.nf
resman \-\-config /etc/my\-config.conf
.fi
.sp
Start with debug logging:
.sp
.nf
LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG resman
.fi
.sp
Check version:
.sp
.nf
resman \-\-version
.fi
.SH SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Linux kernel 4.5+ (cgroups v2)
.IP \(bu
Write access to /sys/fs/cgroup
.IP \(bu
Root privileges or CAP_SYS_ADMIN capability
.IP \(bu
Go runtime (if running from source)
